<worksheet>
    <title>LT 1-2 Tortoise and Hare
    <subtitle>Adapted from Amplify Classroom and "Beginning Algebra Made Useful"</subtitle>
</title>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The tortoise and the hare are racing. The tortoise starts at the starting line and runs at a constant speed.  The hare also starts at the starting line but runs at a faster pace for several minutes.  He then realizes that the tortoise is slow, so he stops to take a nap.  When he wakes up, he sees that the tortoise has passed him, so he starts running again to the finish line.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Use the information provided to answer the following questions:
            </p>
    </statement>

     <statement>
            <p> Which animal does each function represent in the graph?  Label the graph with "Tortoise" and "Hare".  
            </p>
            <p> Explain your thinking.</p>
        <figure>
                <image source="tandhare.png" width="40%">
                    <shortdescription>A distance and time graph with a constant increasing graph starting at (0,0), and a linear graph that increases, then is constant, and ends increasing.</shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>
            
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.05in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the tortoises speed?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.05in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the hare's average speed? (find all three speeds and divide by three)
            </p>
        </statement>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Who won the race?  How do you know?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>    
        <statement>
            <p>
                This graph shows the relationship between the distance and time for a third animal, the fox.
            </p>   
        
        </statement>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Tell a story about the fox's journey during the race.  Include specific details about time and distance.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-fox-graph">
            <image source="tandhareandfox.png" width="40%">
              <shortdescription>A distance and time graph with a constant graph starting at (9,0), a linear graph that increases, then is constant, and ends increasing, and a third graph that starts at (6,0) and is constant</shortdescription>  
            </image>        
        </figure>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.05in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the fox's speed?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Here are five statements about the race.  Select all the true statements.
            </p>
        
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A. The fox's distance was always increasing.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    B.  Between 6 and 10 minutes, the fox was traveling faster than the other animals.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    C.  When the hare reached 800 meters, the fox was still at the starting line.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            LT 1: We will now be drawing the stories
        </p>
    </introduction>
<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
           Next the tortoise raced the dog. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Draw a graph representing the relationship between distance and time for a dog that makes <em>all</em> of these statements true.
        </p>
    
       <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                The dog got a head start but lost the race.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                The dog and tortoise were tied at 600 meters
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                The dog's distance was decreasing for 3 minutes.
            </p>
        </li>
        </ul>
        <figure>
            <image source="tortoise.png" width="40%">
                <shortdescription>Time and distance graph with tortoise's steady race graphed from (0,0)</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </statement>
</task>
<task workspace="0.05in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            The graph shows what Wyatt drew to represent the dog's race.  How did she do?  If it is not correct, tell her story.
        </p>
            At least one of the following statements is false  Circle the false statement.
        <p>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A. The dog got a head start but lost the race
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The dog and tortoise were tied at 800 meters.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The dog's distance was decreasing for 3 minutes.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <figure>
            <image source="tanddog.png" width="40%">
                 <shortdescription>Time and distance graph with tortoise graphed and dog's graph starts at (2,0) and increases for a while, then is constant for several minutes, and then increases again.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </statement>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Explain your thinking
        </p>
    </statement>
</task>
</exercise>
</worksheet>
